From: Waltteri Hooli <wahooli@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] local: drm/msm/dpu: recover a ctl stuck in a hardware-initiated reset

The cold-boot blue screen is CTL_0 sitting in CTL_SW_RESET while the INTF_5
timing engine runs. Three saved snapshots (2026-09-18 x2, 2026-09-19) agree:
SW_RESET=1 within one frame of the engine starting, FLUSH still holding the
DMA0|LM_0|LM_1|CTL bits, FRAME_COUNT advancing, and the panel painting
INTF_UNDERFLOW_COLOR (0xff, blue) because the ctl path delivers nothing.

The driver did not start that reset. 0212 instruments the only writer of the
bit and stayed silent through three stuck kickoffs on 2026-09-19, and on a cold
boot no reset_control() caller is reachable before the first enable anyway. So
this is the "hw initiated ctl reset" the kickoff check was written for, and it
never completes. The ctl's other words are identical to a healthy capture
(dpu-diff 2026-09-19), so nothing there says what it waits on.

Until the trigger is known, recover instead of failing every frame forever:

  1. re-issue the software reset under the running engine;
  2. stop the timing engine, wait for INTF_STATUS to drop, reset again,
     restart the engine.

Each step logs its outcome, so a cold-boot loop says which one clears it, or
that neither does -- which points the next experiment at CTL_SW_RESET_OVERRIDE
(0x060, downstream's hard reset; 0 in every capture). A software reset keeps
the ctl's programming (upstream's own disable path clears INTF_ACTIVE by hand
after one), so nothing is re-applied.

Upstream's failure branch (snapshot, drop the vsync irq) is kept for the case
where both steps fail. Expected on a healthy boot: nothing. Supersedes the
repair half of 0143, whose write of 0 never cleared the bit.

--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/disp/dpu1/dpu_encoder_phys_vid.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/disp/dpu1/dpu_encoder_phys_vid.c
@@ -14,6 +14,7 @@
 
 #include <drm/display/drm_dsc_helper.h>
 #include <drm/drm_managed.h>
+#include <linux/delay.h>
 
 #define DPU_DEBUG_VIDENC(e, fmt, ...) DPU_DEBUG("enc%d intf%d " fmt, \
 		(e) && (e)->parent ? \
@@ -549,6 +550,66 @@
 	return 0;
 }
 
+/*
+ * The ctl is sitting in a software reset the hardware started on its own and
+ * never finished: the driver never wrote the bit (0143/0212), it is set within
+ * one frame of the timing engine starting, and the engine keeps counting frames
+ * while the panel shows INTF_UNDERFLOW_COLOR. Nothing in the ctl's registers
+ * says what it waits for, so escalate and log which step clears it. A software
+ * reset preserves the ctl's programming (the disable path clears INTF_ACTIVE by
+ * hand after one), so nothing is re-applied afterwards.
+ */
+static int dpu_encoder_phys_vid_recover_ctl(struct dpu_encoder_phys *phys_enc)
+{
+	struct dpu_hw_ctl *ctl = phys_enc->hw_ctl;
+	struct dpu_hw_intf *intf = phys_enc->hw_intf;
+	struct dpu_hw_intf_status status = { 0 };
+	unsigned long lock_flags;
+	int rc, i;
+
+	if (!ctl->ops.reset)
+		return -EINVAL;
+
+	/* 1: re-issue the reset under the running timing engine. */
+	rc = ctl->ops.reset(ctl);
+	if (!rc) {
+		DPU_ERROR_VIDENC(phys_enc,
+				 "ctl %d: stuck reset cleared by re-issuing it\n",
+				 ctl->idx);
+		return 0;
+	}
+
+	if (!intf || !intf->ops.enable_timing || !intf->ops.get_status)
+		return rc;
+
+	/*
+	 * 2: stop the engine, let the frame in flight drain (one frame is
+	 * 17 ms at 60 Hz), reset again, restart the engine.
+	 */
+	spin_lock_irqsave(phys_enc->enc_spinlock, lock_flags);
+	intf->ops.enable_timing(intf, 0);
+	spin_unlock_irqrestore(phys_enc->enc_spinlock, lock_flags);
+
+	for (i = 0; i < 40; i++) {
+		intf->ops.get_status(intf, &status);
+		if (!status.is_en)
+			break;
+		usleep_range(1000, 1500);
+	}
+
+	rc = ctl->ops.reset(ctl);
+
+	spin_lock_irqsave(phys_enc->enc_spinlock, lock_flags);
+	intf->ops.enable_timing(intf, 1);
+	spin_unlock_irqrestore(phys_enc->enc_spinlock, lock_flags);
+
+	DPU_ERROR_VIDENC(phys_enc,
+			 "ctl %d: stuck reset %s with the timing engine stopped (engine %s after %d ms)\n",
+			 ctl->idx, rc ? "STILL stuck" : "cleared",
+			 status.is_en ? "still running" : "stopped", i);
+	return rc;
+}
+
 static void dpu_encoder_phys_vid_prepare_for_kickoff(
 		struct dpu_encoder_phys *phys_enc)
 {
@@ -567,6 +628,8 @@
 	 * frame, need to check and wait for hw initiated ctl reset completion
 	 */
 	rc = ctl->ops.wait_reset_status(ctl);
+	if (rc)
+		rc = dpu_encoder_phys_vid_recover_ctl(phys_enc);
 	if (rc) {
 		DPU_ERROR_VIDENC(phys_enc, "ctl %d reset failure: %d\n",
 				ctl->idx, rc);
